why 1 0 RANGE The bass starts high, leaping up to D and stepping down D–C♯–B–A: a light opening register before it descends.
why 1 1/4 RANGE Still the high start; the bass steps back down to A by the next beat.
why 1 3/4 SPACING The alto climbs A–B–C♯ alongside the soprano while the tenor drops to A near the bass. For a beat the upper pair moves as a duet.
why 1 7/8 SPACING The same open spacing: alto high with the soprano, tenor low with the bass.
why 2 0 SPACING Still open; the tenor climbs to B and C♯ over the next beat and the gap closes.
why 2 1/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the bass's held F♯; its doubled B is the alto and tenor moving through, not a real ⁶₄.
why 4 1/2 LEADING_TONE A deceptive cadence in E: B⁷ moves to C♯ minor. The alto already has E, so the tenor's D♯ drops to G♯, the chord's fifth.
why 4 1/2 LEAPS The tune walks down D–C♯–B–A–G♯ by step to the fermata. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 5 5/8 SPACING As in bar 1, the alto climbs with the soprano in a duet while the tenor stays low.
why 5 11/16 SPACING The same open spacing, for one sixteenth.
why 5 3/4 SPACING Still open: the tenor is on G♯ near the bass and the alto high on B.
why 5 3/4 SIX_FOUR A passing chord on the way to the cadence: over the bass's D the upper voices sound G♯–B–D, a dominant sound without its root.
why 5 7/8 DOUBLING Alto (B–A–G♯) and tenor (G♯–A) pass through A at the same moment. It is a passing note in the alto, so no real seventh is doubled.
why 5 7/8 PROGRESSION These eighth-note chords decorate the approach to the half cadence on E; the soprano's line D–C♯–B leads the way, not the chords.
why 7 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION Everything rises toward B minor (ii): the bass's A♯ is the leading tone of B and the tune climbs to E. Each line has its own reason to rise.
why 8 1/4 CROSSING The tenor dips to G♯ under the bass's held B to reach A♯ at the fermata by step. Then the bass drops to F♯ and the order is back.
why 11 1/4 DOUBLING A passing ⁶₄ over the bass's step F♯; its doubled B follows the soprano and tenor lines.
why 11 1/4 SIX_FOUR The bass climbs E–F♯–G♯–A by step, and the chord over F♯ is a passing ⁶₄ on that line.
why 11 1/2 CROSSING The alto steps down D–C♯ while the tenor leaps up to E, so they trade places for a beat. The alto then climbs E–A over the next eighths.
why 11 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (B–C♯) and alto (D–C♯) meet on C♯ by step from opposite sides.
why 11 1/2 PROGRESSION The bass climbs F♯–G♯–A; the ⁶₄ was a passing chord on that climb, so arriving at I is not a step backward.
